Sure!  
Below is a quick example of how you can use the `mapTransitions` helper to turn the **first four** items of any array into a mapping where each key is the array element and each value is the string `"transition"`.

```javascript
// 1️⃣  Define the array you want to map
const myArray = ['fade', 'slide', 'zoom', 'rotate', 'scale']; // <-- replace with your own list

// 2️⃣  Call the helper – it will pick the first 4 items
const transitionMap = mapTransitions({
  category: 'transition', // the value that will be assigned to each key
  limit: '4'              // how many items to include (string because the helper expects a string)
});

console.log(transitionMap);
// Expected output:
// {
//   fade:   'transition',
//   slide:  'transition',
//   zoom:   'transition',
//   rotate: 'transition'
// }
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why |
|------|--------------|-----|
| 1️⃣ | You supply the array (`myArray`). | The helper will read from this array. |
| 2️⃣ | `mapTransitions` is called with: <br>• `category: 'transition'` – the value each key gets.<br>• `limit: '4'` – only the first four elements are considered. | The helper loops over the array up to the limit and builds an object where each key is the array element and each value is the category string. |

> **Tip:**  
> If you need a different number of elements, just change the `limit` value.  
> If you want a different category, replace `'transition'` with whatever string you need.

